### Dana's first customer in Acme. A person names the tenant she acts in with `X-Alvo-Tenant`, and a scoped create
### needs nothing more: the body carries no `tenant_id`, and the row is placed in the tenant the call acts in (#538).
# @name DanaCreatesCustomer
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/customers
Authorization: Bearer {{DanaAccess}}
X-Alvo-Tenant: {{AcmeId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Acme's first customer",
  "tier": "standard"
}

### The same create naming a tenant other than the one the call acts in — here the region's id, which is no tenant
### of Dana's. Refused before any row is read, with a detail that names the field and the fix and never repeats the
### value sent.
# @name DanaCreatesInAnotherTenant
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [403]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/customers
Authorization: Bearer {{DanaAccess}}
X-Alvo-Tenant: {{AcmeId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{RegionId}}",
  "name": "Acme's first customer",
  "tier": "standard"
}

### A job for Theo: scheduled, assigned to him
# @name DanaCreatesTheosJob
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
Authorization: Bearer {{DanaAccess}}
X-Alvo-Tenant: {{AcmeId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{AcmeId}}",
  "reference": "{{TheoJobReference}}",
  "title": "Replace the kitchen tap",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 2,
  "scheduled_for": "{{TomorrowMorning}}",
  "assigned_to": "{{TheoId}}",
  "access_code": "ACME-TAP",
  "customer_id": "{{DanaCreatesCustomer.response.body.$.id}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionId}}"
}

### A job for nobody yet, in the same tenant
# @name DanaCreatesOtherJob
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
Authorization: Bearer {{DanaAccess}}
X-Alvo-Tenant: {{AcmeId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{AcmeId}}",
  "reference": "{{OtherJobReference}}",
  "title": "Service the boiler",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 3,
  "access_code": "ACME-BOILER",
  "customer_id": "{{DanaCreatesCustomer.response.body.$.id}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionId}}"
}

### Theo's list in Acme: the row rule (`assigned_to == @user.id`) narrows it, his tenant role does not widen it
# @name TheoListsInAcme
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?order=reference.asc
Authorization: Bearer {{TheoAccess}}
X-Alvo-Tenant: {{AcmeId}}

### The same read naming no tenant. Theo has no personal tenant (the descriptor declares none), so he acts in none,
### and a scoped entity answers the tenant guard rather than an empty page — or a page of anyone's rows.
# @name TheoListsWithoutTenant
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [403]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?order=reference.asc
Authorization: Bearer {{TheoAccess}}

### A technician cannot place a job. Every job names a customer, and `customers` admits no technician to read one, so
### the reference does not resolve for him — answered before the row rule, as a 422 at /customer_id: a row he cannot
### read is indistinguishable from one that does not exist. (Dana's 201 with the same customer_id is the control.)
# @name TheoCreatesAJob
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [422]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
Authorization: Bearer {{TheoAccess}}
X-Alvo-Tenant: {{AcmeId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{AcmeId}}",
  "reference": "{{OtherJobReference}}9",
  "title": "A job Theo would like",
  "status": "new",
  "priority": 5,
  "access_code": "ACME-THEO",
  "customer_id": "{{DanaCreatesCustomer.response.body.$.id}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionId}}"
}

### The `create` rule itself, where nothing is left to resolve first: a customer carries no reference, so the row
### rule (`'dispatcher' in @user.roles || 'admin' in @user.roles`) is what answers, and a technician holds neither
# @name TheoCreatesACustomer
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [403]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/customers
Authorization: Bearer {{TheoAccess}}
X-Alvo-Tenant: {{AcmeId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{AcmeId}}",
  "name": "Theo's own customer",
  "tier": "standard"
}

### He arrives on site: `start` admits a technician, and the job is his
# @name TheoStarts
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
PATCH {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{DanaCreatesTheosJob.response.body.$.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{TheoAccess}}
X-Alvo-Tenant: {{AcmeId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "in_progress" }

### And signs it off: `complete` demands the day
# @name TheoCompletes
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
PATCH {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{DanaCreatesTheosJob.response.body.$.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{TheoAccess}}
X-Alvo-Tenant: {{AcmeId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "completed", "completed_on": "{{Today}}" }

### Dana's list in Acme — the control: both jobs exist, so Theo's single row is the rule, not the data
# @name DanaListsInAcme
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?order=reference.asc
Authorization: Bearer {{DanaAccess}}
X-Alvo-Tenant: {{AcmeId}}
